Title = {Disentangling conservation asymmetries through socio-economic
   transboundary factors across the Alto Paraná Atlantic Forest of South
   America},
Journal = {PEOPLE AND NATURE},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {8},
Number = {5},
Pages = {1328-1348},
Month = {MAY},
Abstract = {Ecoregions are often defined based on homogeneous biophysical and
   ecological conditions and are optimal spatial units for designing
   conservation strategies. However, transboundary ecoregions such as the
   Alto Paran \& aacute; Atlantic Forest (APAF) experience asymmetrical
   conservation outcomes, understood here as cross-border differences,
   resulting from national land-use policies and socio-economic
   trajectories. We integrated literature review and spatial analysis of
   social and ecological variables, such as population density, forest
   fragmentation, or protection levels, using an interdisciplinary
   approach. We measured conservation and socio-economic asymmetries by
   calculating pairwise Euclidean distances amongst these variables per
   pair of countries in the APAF ecoregion: Argentina, Brazil and Paraguay.
   We also generated and compared conservation and human development
   scores. Despite being part of the same ecoregion, national borders have
   shaped distinct social-ecological systems with contrasting priorities
   and outcomes. Argentina was the most distinct system amongst APAF
   countries in terms of land use and forest conservation, while Brazil and
   Paraguay shared more similarities. This reflects better forest
   conservation in Argentina, although demographic, cultural and economic
   differences amongst the three countries are less pronounced. In
   political terms, Argentina and Brazil showed greater similarity,
   although all three countries have enacted policies to reduce
   deforestation; forest cover continues to decline throughout the region.
   Additionally, our findings showed that in the Argentine APAF, forest
   conservation and human development, beyond economic metrics, are
   compatible goals. Our results suggest that integrated approaches that
   combine ecological data with historical and socio-political factors are
   needed to guide effective cross-border forest conservation and
   sustainable development. We recommend incorporating local voices to
   capture cultural nuances and lived experiences to enrich the
   understanding of human-nature interactions, while fostering the
   international cooperation that reinforces conservation strategies in
   Argentina and prioritise ecological restoration in Brazil and
   Paraguay.Read the free for this article on the Journal blog.
   Las ecorregiones suelen definirse en funci \& oacute;n de condiciones
   biof \& iacute;sicas y ecol \& oacute;gicas homog \& eacute;neas, y
   constituyen unidades espaciales \& oacute;ptimas para dise \& ntilde;ar
   estrategias de conservaci \& oacute;n. Sin embargo, en las ecorregiones
   transfronterizas como el Bosque Atl \& aacute;ntico del Alto Paran \&
   aacute; (BAAP) se pueden presentar asimetr \& iacute;as, entendidas aqu
   \& iacute; como diferencias transfronterizas resultantes de las pol \&
   iacute;ticas nacionales de uso del suelo y las trayectorias socioecon \&
   oacute;micas Integramos revisi \& oacute;n bibliogr \& aacute;fica y an
   \& aacute;lisis espacial de variables sociales y ecol \& oacute;gicas,
   como densidad poblacional, fragmentaci \& oacute;n forestal o niveles de
   protecci \& oacute;n, mediante un enfoque interdisciplinario. Medimos
   las asimetr \& iacute;as en conservaci \& oacute;n y en el desarrollo
   socio-econ \& oacute;mico calculando distancias euclidianas pareadas
   entre estas variables para cada par de pa \& iacute;ses que comparten la
   ecorregi \& oacute;n: Argentina, Brasil y Paraguay. Tambi \& eacute;n
   generamos y comparamos los \& iacute;ndices de conservaci \& oacute;n y
   desarrollo humano. A pesar de formar parte de la misma ecorregi \&
   oacute;n, las fronteras nacionales moldearon los sistemas socio-ecol \&
   oacute;gicos de cada pa \& iacute;s con prioridades y resultados
   diferentes. Argentina result \& oacute; ser el sistema m \& aacute;s
   diferente en t \& eacute;rminos de uso del suelo y conservaci \&
   oacute;n forestal, mientras que Brasil y Paraguay mostraron mayores
   similitudes. Esto refleja un mejor estado de conservaci \& oacute;n para
   el BAAP en Argentina, aunque las diferencias demogr \& aacute;ficas,
   culturales y econ \& oacute;micas entre los tres pa \& iacute;ses son
   menos marcadas. En t \& eacute;rminos pol \& iacute;ticos, Argentina y
   Brasil exhibieron mayor similitud; y aunque los tres pa \& iacute;ses
   han promulgado pol \& iacute;ticas para reducir la deforestaci \&
   oacute;n, la cobertura forestal sigue disminuyendo en toda la regi \&
   oacute;n. Adem \& aacute;s, nuestros resultados mostraron que en la
   porci \& oacute;n argentina del BAAP, la conservaci \& oacute;n y el
   desarrollo humano, m \& aacute;s all \& aacute; de los indicadores econ
   \& oacute;micos, son objetivos compatibles. Sugerimos la implementaci \&
   oacute;n de enfoques integrados que combinen datos ecol \& oacute;gicos
   con factores hist \& oacute;ricos y socio-pol \& iacute;ticos para guiar
   una conservaci \& oacute;n transfronteriza eficaz y un desarrollo
   sostenible. Finalmente, recomendamos incorporar voces locales para
   capturar matices culturales y experiencias de vida que enriquezcan la
   comprensi \& oacute;n de las interacciones entre los seres humanos y la
   naturaleza, y fomentar al mismo tiempo la cooperaci \& oacute;n
   internacional para reforzar las estrategias de conservaci \& oacute;n en
   Argentina y priorizar la restauraci \& oacute;n ecol \& oacute;gica en
   Brasil y Paraguay.},
